---
// Home cards (on the Home screen's page, screens/[id].astro): choosing what is on Home + the card sizes. Facts:
// core/spec.h CARD_TYPES (types, taps) + grid_sizes (1x1 2x1 2x2 half), screens/home.cpp (the Grid, unavailable look), screens/screens.cpp
// + screen_set.cpp (Settings > Screens: every built-in screen, always on the panel), renderer/README.md "Feature flags" (cards to screens the build
// lacks are left out). Only released card types.
import { url } from '../lib/nav';
import Shot from './Shot.astro';

const sizes = [
  ['1 × 1', 'One square', 'A tile: the icon and the name side by side at the top, the state at the bottom, such as a light and its level.'],
  ['Half', 'Half a square', 'The icon and the name over the state. Two halves share one square, one above the other.'],
  ['2 × 1', 'Two squares across', 'The card with its picture, such as the animated icon for Weather, and a line under it.'],
  ['2 × 2', 'Four squares', 'The card with room to spare, such as the record and the track for Media.'],
];
const types = [
  ['Light (or a switch)', 'A tap toggles it, opens the Light screen for it, or does nothing.'],
  ['Media player', 'Shows the track and the album art on a record that turns while it plays; a tap opens the Media screen.'],
  ['Sensor', 'Read only: the value and its label.'],
  ['State readout (half only)', 'A sensor\'s state with its icon and no box, in the accent colour while it is on; no tap.'],
  ['Screen', 'Opens another of your screens or a built-in one.'],
  ['Any other entity', 'Climate, a cover, a fan, a script, a scene, a button and the rest: what a tap does depends on what it is.'],
];
---

  <h2 id="cards">Home cards</h2>
  <p>
    Home is the screen the panel starts on: a grid of cards, each one a
    light, a speaker, a sensor or a way into another screen. The cards keep their order and fill the grid row by
    row, and the page scrolls when there are more than fit. A panel can have more than one Home, for example one per
    room, with a card on one that opens the next.
  </p>

  <h3 id="sizes">Card sizes</h3>
  <p>Home is a grid three squares across, the same on the 480 and the 720 panel. Every card can be any of these sizes; the <a href={url('/editor/#sizes')}>editor</a> sets them.</p>
  <figure class="page-shot" style="max-width: 420px"><Shot id="home" alt="A Home with a 2 by 1 Weather card, a 1 by 1 Clock card, a 2 by 2 Media card, a 1 by 1 light card and two half sensor cards." /><figcaption>2 × 1 and 1 × 1, 2 × 2 and 1 × 1, then two halves</figcaption></figure>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Shape</th><th>Size</th><th>What it is for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {sizes.map(([name, size, about]) => <tr><td><strong>{name}</strong></td><td>{size}</td><td>{about}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 id="types">What a card can show</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Card</th><th>Tap</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {types.map(([name, tap]) => <tr><td>{name}</td><td>{tap}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The label is the one you give the card, or else the entity's name from Home Assistant. A card only appears when its
    screen is part of your panel's firmware, so a card never leads to a screen that is not there.
  </p>

  <h3 id="choose">Choosing what is on Home</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>In the <a href={url('/editor/')}>editor</a></strong> (the panel's own web page, <code>http://&lt;the
      panel's address&gt;/</code>): add, resize, move and remove cards on a live copy of the panel, then save. A fresh
      panel starts with one empty Home.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>On the panel</strong> (<strong>Settings &gt; Screens</strong>): every built-in screen, with where its data
      comes from. Every one is always on the panel, with no switch: its card on Home is added and removed in the editor
      like any other card, and a screen reads its data only while it shows or a card for it is on Home. Tap a screen's name to set its
      source and options, such as which speaker the Media screen follows.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your own cards</strong>: the full list of Homes, cards and screens is the panel's <em>deck</em>. An AI
      assistant can make one for your entities and send it to the panel; <a href={url('/ai/')}>Make screens with AI</a>
      shows how.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="unavailable">When an entity is unavailable</h3>
  <p>
    If Home Assistant reports an entity as unavailable, its card stays in place but greys out: the icon and label dim,
    the value shows a dash and the sub line reads <strong>Unavailable</strong>. A tap only plays a short error sound.
    When the entity is back, the card returns by itself.
  </p>
